WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:14.777
In this tutorial, we'll test a native app the way a screen reader, or an AI agent, uses it: by the labels on its controls. We'll read the code in the native and uitest packages, run real tests, and break one on purpose.

00:15.527 --> 00:19.099
There are two ways for software to use an interface it didn't write.

00:19.299 --> 00:24.006
It can look at pixels: take a screenshot, guess where the Play button is, and click there.

00:24.206 --> 00:31.187
Or it can read the accessibility tree. Every control has a role, like button or slider, and a label, like Play.

00:31.387 --> 00:36.345
The button labelled Play survives a redesign that moves it. A pixel guess doesn't.

00:37.095 --> 00:45.528
This is the casting player from the Chromecast tutorial. Every control you see is in its accessibility tree, and its tests never look at a pixel.

00:46.278 --> 00:57.295
Labels start in the app's machine document, the data file that describes the whole interface. This is one hit area: a rectangle on the canvas, sized for the Open file button.

00:57.495 --> 01:01.216
When it's pressed, it fires the open event, which the machine handles.

01:01.416 --> 01:10.240
And here are its role and label: a button, labelled Open file. That one line is all a test, a screen reader or an agent needs to find it.

01:10.989 --> 01:26.031
The native package turns those hit areas into a typed tree. Semantics walks every live listener, and gives each one its role, its filled-in label, its bounds, and a slider's value and range. A hidden control isn't live, so it isn't in the tree.

01:26.231 --> 01:36.857
Act operates a control by label, the way a screen reader would. A slider is set a fraction of the way across, and anything else is pressed. An unknown label changes nothing.

01:37.607 --> 01:49.790
The uitest package builds on that tree. It's published as @osprey/uitest, under MIT: label-driven UI testing for Osprey Native apps, with no host and no window.

01:49.990 --> 01:54.287
It depends on the native, graphics and json packages, and nothing else.

01:55.037 --> 02:01.779
Semantics reads the tree. Labels joins every label in hit order, so one string check covers the whole screen.

02:01.979 --> 02:11.730
Element finds a control by its label. If nothing has that label, it returns an element with no bounds, instead of crashing, so the test can report the problem.

02:11.930 --> 02:17.345
Role and value read what a host would announce: button, slider, and a slider's current value.

02:17.545 --> 02:23.497
And point turns a label into a place to click: a fraction of the way across the control, halfway down.

02:24.247 --> 02:32.219
Announced writes one element as an agent reads it: the role, the label in quotes, a slider's value and range, and whether it's selected.

02:32.419 --> 02:41.050
Outline writes the whole tree, one line each. That's exactly the text an agent gets, and, as you'll see, exactly what a failing test prints.

02:41.800 --> 02:47.934
Drive holds a live session. A Driven value is the session, what it last rendered, and the host's clock.

02:48.134 --> 02:55.577
Press builds a host event for the control with that label. The test never deals with revision numbers or internal ordinals.

02:55.777 --> 03:02.250
Pointer builds a raw pointer event at a point on the canvas, pressed or released. That's what a real click is.

03:03.000 --> 03:08.411
Every check explains itself when it fails. Explained prints the reason, then returns false.

03:08.611 --> 03:17.390
Offers checks the tree has a label, and if it doesn't, it prints the whole tree as an agent would read it. So a failure tells you what the screen does offer.

03:18.140 --> 03:27.840
Now a real test, from the casting player. A few one-line helpers read the tree from what the app last rendered: its elements, their labels, a slider's value.

03:28.040 --> 03:40.447
Tap at finds the control by label, takes its point, and sends a real pointer press and release there. If a hit area is misplaced, or covered by something else, the tap misses, just as a user's click would.

03:41.197 --> 03:52.377
Read the journey like a user would. Before any TVs are found, the screen offers four controls. Once the fake TV answers, the receiver label changes to Living Room TV.

03:52.577 --> 03:59.757
Tap Play with no file, and nothing is sent to the TV. Tap Open file, and the app asks the host for a file picker.

03:59.957 --> 04:06.644
Pick a file, tap Play, and the fake TV hears CONNECT and LAUNCH. A few frames later, the canvas says Playing.

04:06.844 --> 04:18.756
Now the tree has changed: Play became Pause and Stop, and two sliders appeared, Position and Volume. The test checks each slider's range, and that Position is a slider and Pause is a button.

04:19.506 --> 04:31.038
Run the test folder with osprey. There's no window and no TV: the app's real start and dispatch run against handlers, so it takes seconds, and runs anywhere. The canvas journey passes.

04:31.238 --> 04:38.761
So does the journey that reads the tree as an agent would: while it searches, when it finds no TVs, and when it finds some.

04:38.961 --> 04:39.958
All eight pass.

04:40.708 --> 04:50.459
Now change one thing. In the machine document, rename this label from Open file to just Open. The button looks the same. Only its label changes.

04:51.209 --> 05:00.171
Run the tests again. Three journeys fail: the skins test, the canvas test, and the agent test. Five still pass, because they never use that button.

05:00.371 --> 05:06.330
The first failure shows the labels it expected, and the labels it got. Open file became Open.

05:06.530 --> 05:19.320
And offers prints the tree an agent would see: two popups and two buttons, Play and Open. A redesign that keeps the labels passes. A renamed label is caught, because agents and screen readers depend on it.

05:20.070 --> 05:38.489
The same tree reaches real assistive technology. The GPUI desktop host maps each element to AccessKit, the cross-platform accessibility layer, and turns a screen reader's click into the same pointer events the tests send. So the app can't tell a screen reader or an agent from a hand.

05:38.689 --> 05:46.229
The role function maps each role name to its AccessKit role. Anything unknown gets no role, so it's never announced wrongly.

05:46.979 --> 06:01.810
What's done, and what isn't. The labels agents use are tested on every build. There's no ready-made MCP server for agents yet. The tree comes from UI machines, not hand-written views, and only the GPUI host maps it today.

06:02.560 --> 06:14.939
To try it, run the casting journeys with osprey, read the uitest package, and open the player to see the same controls. Osprey is actively evolving. Try it on your projects, and tell us what breaks.
